CAT = "rsracCommission"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("Why is a compressor's crankcase heater energized for hours before a first start in cold weather?",
 "Drives refrigerant out of the oil", ["Warms the motor windings to start", "Raises the suction pressure", "Thins the refrigerant charge"],
 "Refrigerant migrates to the cold crankcase and dissolves in the oil. Starting then makes the oil foam and wash out of the bearings; the heater boils the refrigerant out first."),
("At start-up, a three-phase scroll compressor is noisy and draws low current. What is the likely cause?",
 "Running backward", ["Overcharged with oil", "Low supply voltage", "Dirty condenser coil"],
 "A scroll turning backward does not pump, so suction and discharge pressures barely change, current is low and it is noisy. Swapping two supply legs corrects the rotation."),
("A system's evaporator saturation is 4 C and the suction line at the coil outlet reads 12 C. What is the superheat?",
 "8 K", ["16 K", "4 K", "12 K"],
 "Superheat is the actual vapour temperature minus the saturation temperature at that pressure: 12 - 4 = 8 K."),
("Condensing saturation is 41 C and the liquid line at the condenser outlet reads 34 C. What is the subcooling?",
 "7 K", ["34 K", "75 K", "41 K"],
 "Subcooling is the saturation temperature minus the actual liquid temperature: 41 - 34 = 7 K."),
("On a system with a thermostatic expansion valve, which reading is the usual target for setting the charge?",
 "Subcooling at the condenser", ["Superheat at the coil outlet", "Compressor running amperage", "Outdoor fan speed"],
 "A TXV holds superheat itself, so superheat says little about charge. Subcooling shows how much liquid backs up in the condenser and is the maker's usual charging target."),
("On a fixed-orifice system, how is the charge usually checked?",
 "Superheat from the chart", ["Subcooling at the condenser", "A clear sight glass", "Running amps vs nameplate"],
 "With a fixed metering device, superheat changes with charge and load, so the maker's chart gives a target superheat for the indoor wet bulb and outdoor temperature."),
("Why is a sight glass full of bubbles not proof of an undercharge on a system with a receiver?",
 "Flash gas has other causes", ["Sight glasses always show bubbles", "Receivers hold vapour only", "Bubbles mean too much oil"],
 "Bubbles appear with a restricted drier, low head pressure in cold weather or a sight glass upstream of a pressure drop. Charge is confirmed with subcooling and superheat."),
("Before a new rooftop unit is started, why is the supply voltage measured at the disconnect?",
 "Motors need voltage within their rating", ["To set the thermostat schedule", "To size the refrigerant charge", "To find the line set length"],
 "Motors are rated for a voltage range. Running outside it overheats windings, so supply voltage, and phase balance on three-phase, are checked before start."),
("Three-phase supply readings are 600, 588 and 612 V. What is the voltage imbalance?",
 "2%", ["4%", "1%", "12%"],
 "Average is 600 V. The largest deviation is 12 V, and 12 / 600 = 2%. Many motor makers want imbalance at or below 2%."),
("After start-up, the compressor's running current reads well above its rated load amps. What does that suggest?",
 "A fault that must be investigated", ["Normal for a new compressor", "Low refrigerant charge only", "Incorrect thermostat setting"],
 "Current far over RLA means the motor is overloaded by high head pressure, overcharge, a mechanical problem or bad voltage. It is investigated before the unit is left running."),
("Why is the expansion valve's superheat adjusted only in small steps on a refrigeration system?",
 "The system needs time to settle", ["The valve breaks if turned quickly", "Each turn adds refrigerant", "The stem leaks when moved"],
 "After each adjustment the coil and compressor take several minutes to reach a new balance. Large turns lead to hunting and floodback."),
("Which evacuation result shows a system is ready to charge?",
 "Vacuum that holds on the decay test", ["Gauge needle at zero psig", "Pump run for a full hour", "No frost left on the hoses"],
 "Time on the pump is not proof. A micron gauge must show a deep vacuum that stays within the maker's limit once the pump is isolated."),
("A split system's line set is longer than its factory charge covers. How is the charge completed?",
 "Weigh it in, then verify", ["Release some to be safe", "Charge until the line sweats", "Run it a day before checking"],
 "Weigh in any extra charge for the line length first, then confirm with superheat or subcooling at stable conditions."),
("During start-up of a refrigerated display case, why is the defrost schedule set?",
 "To clear frost from the coil", ["To raise the case temperature daily", "To test the door switches", "To dry the drain line"],
 "Frost builds on low-temperature coils and blocks airflow. The defrost interval and termination are set to the maker's values for the product and conditions."),
("A cooling tower's water treatment is started at commissioning. What does it mainly control?",
 "Scale and bacteria", ["Fan motor noise", "Refrigerant pressure", "Electrical demand"],
 "Treatment and bleed-off keep minerals from scaling the condenser tubes, slow corrosion and control biological growth such as Legionella."),
("Why is the indoor blower speed checked when a new split system is commissioned?",
 "Airflow must match the coil's design", ["It sets the refrigerant type", "It changes the line set size", "It controls the defrost cycle"],
 "Too little airflow lowers coil temperature and can freeze it; too much reduces dehumidification. The blower tap is set for the maker's airflow at the duct's static pressure."),
("Before starting a large chiller, what is confirmed about its chilled water flow?",
 "Proven by a flow switch before start", ["Set by the cooling tower fan", "Checked after the first hour", "Measured by suction pressure"],
 "A chiller must not run without water flow through the evaporator, or the tubes can freeze. A flow switch or differential pressure switch proves flow before the compressor starts."),
("Why are pressure control cut-in and cut-out values set from the refrigerant's PT chart?",
 "They follow box temperature", ["They set the crankcase oil level", "They size the line set", "They choose the filter-drier"],
 "A pressure control acting as a temperature control works because saturation pressure tracks temperature. The PT chart turns the target box temperatures into settings."),
("What does a non-condensable gas in a new system usually do to head pressure?",
 "Raises it above normal", ["Lowers it below normal", "Leaves it unchanged", "Makes it fall to zero"],
 "Air and nitrogen collect in the condenser, take up surface and add their own pressure, so head pressure runs higher than the condensing temperature explains."),
("When commissioning a ductless heat pump, why is heating mode also run, even in summer?",
 "Tests the reversing valve", ["Burns off factory oil", "Sets the refrigerant charge", "Dries the indoor coil"],
 "Commissioning checks every mode the unit will use, including the reversing valve switching, so a fault is found before the heating season."),
]
